\section{Find each parameter}{
The seven parameter blocks appear in the same order in \code{parameters},
\code{specification$parameters}, \code{draws}, and every concatenated vector or
matrix (the Laplace \code{mode}, \code{covariance}, and \code{hessian}, the importance
\code{candidates}, and the columns of \code{chains}).\tabular{lllll}{
   Block \tab One entry per \tab What it holds \tab Scale stored \tab Interpretable form \cr
   \code{beta} \tab name in \code{specification$beta_names} \tab Response coefficients \tab Link scale \tab Read directly, or \code{exp()} for count and binomial models. Summarized in \code{summary(fit)$coefficients} \cr
   \code{scale_beta} \tab column of each scale design \tab Scale-formula coefficients \tab Log of distance divided by \code{specification$unit_conv} for intercepts and \code{~0 + year} coefficients. Log-distance contrasts for the rest \tab Add \code{log(unit_conv)} to the intercepts to get log map units, then \code{exp()} for a distance. \code{exp()} of a contrast is a ratio of distances. Summarized in \code{summary(fit)$scale_coefficients} \cr
   \code{scale_z} \tab level of each scale grouping term \tab Standardized group deviations with a standard-normal prior \tab None \tab Multiply by the group SD to get the deviation in log scale \cr
   \code{log_scale_sd} \tab scale random-intercept term \tab Log of the between-group SD of log scale (tau) \tab Log \tab \code{exp()} gives tau. \code{exp(tau)} is a multiplier on the scale. Summarized in \code{summary(fit)$group_sd$log_scale_group_sd} \cr
   \code{response_z} \tab level of each response grouping term \tab Standardized random intercepts with a standard-normal prior \tab None \tab Multiply by the response group SD to get the intercept on the link scale \cr
   \code{log_response_sd} \tab response random-intercept term \tab Log of the random-intercept SD \tab Log of a link-scale SD \tab \code{exp()}. Summarized in \code{summary(fit)$group_sd$response_group_sd} \cr
   \code{log_dispersion} \tab NB2 or Gaussian model (empty otherwise) \tab Log NB2 size theta, or log Gaussian residual SD \tab Log \tab \code{exp()}. Summarized in \code{summary(fit)$dispersion} \cr
}


The link scale is the log expected count for Poisson and NB2 models, the
log odds for binomial models, and response units for Gaussian models.

Each landscape predictor's scale for observation \code{i}, in map units, is

\deqn{\log D_i = x_i^\top b + \sum_k \exp(s_k) z_{k, g_k(i)} + \log(u)}{log D[i] = x[i]' b + sum over k of exp(s[k]) z[k, g[k](i)] + log(u)}

where \eqn{x_i} is row \code{i} of the scale design
\code{specification$scales[[name]]$X}, \eqn{b} is
\code{scale_beta[specification$scales[[name]]$beta_index]}, \eqn{s_k} is the
entry of \code{log_scale_sd} for random term \eqn{k}, \eqn{z_{k, g}} is the
entry of \code{scale_z} for group \eqn{g}, and \eqn{u} is
\code{specification$unit_conv}. Within \code{specification$scales[[name]]$random},
each term records \code{z_index} (positions in \code{scale_z}, one per level in
\code{levels}), \code{index} (each row's level), and \code{sd_index} (position in
\code{log_scale_sd}). Response random terms in \code{specification$response$random}
use the same fields for \code{response_z} and \code{log_response_sd}. This
noncentered form, a standard-normal deviation times a group SD, is the
coordinate system the samplers and approximations work in.

For a shared scale, \code{exp(scale_beta + log(unit_conv))} is the scale itself.
For \code{~1 + (1 | year)}, the same expression gives the typical scale, the
median of the population distribution of annual scales, and tau is
\code{exp(log_scale_sd)}.
A year one SD above typical has a scale \code{exp(tau)} times the typical one.
\code{\link[=posterior_scales]{posterior_scales()}} performs these conversions for every distinct
combination of scale covariates and groups. Prefer it to rebuilding them
by hand.
}

\section{Read the specification}{
\code{specification} is a list. Most users need only a few elements.
\itemize{
\item \code{covariates}: names of the landscape predictors whose scales are
estimated. \code{kernel} is \code{"gaussian"} or \code{"exp"}.
\item \code{unit_conv}: the distance normalizer of the prepared inputs. Internal
log scales are in units of distance divided by \code{unit_conv}.
\item \code{support}: \code{min_D} and \code{max_D} of the prepared inputs, in map units. They
are a reference range for \code{\link[=posterior_scales]{posterior_scales()}}, not bounds on the prior.
\item \code{center}, \code{spread}: the fixed reference standardization of each
landscape predictor, computed once across fitting rows at the prior
median scale. A response coefficient refers to one \code{spread} unit of the
kernel-weighted predictor at that scale.
\item \code{priors}: the complete prior settings, with defaults filled in.
\code{scale_median} is a named vector, one entry per landscape predictor.
\item \code{beta_names}: the names of the response coefficients, in \code{beta} order.
\item \code{scales}: one list per landscape predictor, holding the scale \code{formula},
its design matrix \code{X}, \code{beta_index}, \code{distance_location} (\code{TRUE} for
coefficients that are log distances rather than contrasts), the prior
mean and SD of each coefficient (\code{prior_mean}, \code{prior_sd}, on the
internal log scale), \code{random} terms, and \code{labels} with the
\code{representatives} rows used to report each distinct combination.
\item \code{response}: the response formula, its model frame, terms, \code{random}
terms, and the levels and contrasts retained for prediction.
\item \code{family}, \code{y}, \code{trials}, \code{offset}: the response family, the response
vector (successes for binomial data), the binomial trial totals (1 for
other families), and the offset.
\item \code{data}, \code{rows}, \code{observation_ids}, \code{row_id}: the observation data, the
prepared landscape row of each observation, its point ID, and the name of
the row-ID column (\code{NULL} when row names were used). Older saved fits can
lack \code{row_id}.
\item \code{V}, \code{C}, \code{delta}: binned value sums, finite-cell counts, and shifted
distance terms for kernel means.
\item \code{X_fixed}: the design columns that do not involve a landscape predictor.
}

Some models add \code{response_design} (interactions and expressions that
involve a landscape predictor), \code{surface} (weighted SQ buffers, their
specifications, and the \code{memory} planning record), or \code{fixed_reference}
(the preparation normalization of declared fixed-radius metrics).
}

\section{Read the inference metadata}{
\code{inference_metadata} describes the target and the treatment of uncertainty.
It does not report whether a fit passed its checks.
\itemize{
\item \code{schema_version}: version of this structure.
\item \code{target}: always \code{"posterior"} for these objects.
\item \code{method}: \code{"prepare"}, \code{"nuts"}, \code{"slice"}, \code{"joint_laplace"},
\code{"marginal_laplace"}, or \code{"importance_corrected"}.
\item \code{objective}: \code{"negative_log_posterior"}. \code{population_priors} is
\code{"proper"}, because every parameter has a prior. \code{response_normalizers}
is \code{"retained"}.
\item \code{integration}: how scale effects, response effects, and population
parameters (group SDs) are treated, such as \code{"joint_sampling"},
\code{"joint_gaussian"}, or \code{"joint_importance_sampling"}, or
\code{"not_computed"} for a prepared object.
\item \code{solver}: \code{"none"}, \code{"nuts"}, \code{"slice"}, \code{"optimization"}, or
\code{"importance_sampling"}.
\item \code{uncertainty}: \code{intervals} is \code{"marginal_credible"} once fitted and
\code{"not_provided"} when prepared. \code{population_parameters} and
\code{annual_scales} state that group-SD uncertainty is propagated through the
joint posterior. \code{calibration} is always \code{"not_established"}, because no
method has demonstrated interval coverage for general data sets.
\item \code{diagnostics_field}: the name of the element that holds the checks,
\code{"diagnostics"}. \code{metadata_establishes_eligibility} is always \code{FALSE}.
}
}

\section{Read the stored diagnostics}{
Every fit has \code{diagnostics$passed} (\code{TRUE}, \code{FALSE}, or \code{NA}) and, in most
cases, \code{criteria}, a sentence stating the rule applied. The other fields
depend on the method.\tabular{lll}{
   Method \tab Fields \tab \code{passed} requires \cr
   NUTS \tab \code{parameters} (a posterior summary table with \code{rhat}, \code{ess_bulk}, \code{ess_tail}), \code{divergences}, \code{max_treedepth_hits}, \code{ebfmi} (one per chain) \tab At least 2 chains, Rhat at most 1.01, bulk and tail effective sample size at least 100 per chain, no divergences or tree-depth hits, and E-BFMI at least 0.3 \cr
   Slice \tab \code{parameters} \tab The same chain rules, without the NUTS-only checks. Without the posterior package, \code{passed} is \code{NA}, \code{parameters} is \code{NULL}, and \code{reason} explains why \cr
   Laplace \tab \code{type}, \code{gradient_max}, \code{min_eigenvalue}, \code{condition_number}, \code{competing_modes}, \code{successful_starts}, \code{starts} \tab No distinct mode within 2 log-posterior units. A failed optimizer or an invalid Hessian stops the fit instead \cr
   Marginal \tab \code{type}, \code{grid_converged}, \code{grid_nodes}, \code{refinement}, \code{competing_mass}, \code{upper_tail_mass}, \code{gradient_max}, \code{condition_number} \tab A converged grid, \code{competing_mass} at most 0.01, and \code{upper_tail_mass} at most 0.001 \cr
   Importance \tab \code{pareto_k}, \code{ess}, \code{min_tail_ess}, \code{max_weight}, \code{split_cdf_error}, \code{type}, \code{candidates}, \code{min_candidates}, \code{proposal_df}, \code{production_candidates}, \code{proposal_passed} \tab Pareto k at most 0.7, \code{ess} at least 1000, \code{min_tail_ess} at least 400, \code{max_weight} at most 0.01, and \code{split_cdf_error} at most 0.05 \cr
}


In the importance checks, \code{ess} measures how concentrated the weights
are, \code{min_tail_ess} the precision of tail indicators, \code{pareto_k} the
presence of extreme ratios, \code{max_weight} dominance by one candidate, and
\code{split_cdf_error} the disagreement between two independent halves of the
production candidates. \code{min_candidates} is the requested minimum.

For the samplers, these checks cover the raw parameters. Use
\code{\link[=diagnose_bayes_scales]{diagnose_bayes_scales()}} to check the scales and group SDs you report. For
the approximations, a passed check says the arithmetic finished cleanly. It
does not say the approximation is close to the posterior.
\code{vignette("v15-bayesian-computation", package = "multiScaleR")} explains
each check and what to do when one fails.
}

\section{Read the approximation records}{
Modes, covariances, Hessians, and candidates in these records use the
unconstrained, noncentered coordinates of "Find each parameter", with the
blocks concatenated in order.
\itemize{
\item \code{laplace}: \code{mode}, the joint \code{covariance} and \code{hessian} at the mode,
every optimizer attempt in \code{starts}, the \code{selected_start}, and the number
of \code{draws}, \code{seed}, \code{cores}, and a \code{coordinates} note. The covariance
keeps correlations among coefficients, scales, and group SDs.
\item \code{marginal}: the integration grid over the one scale-group SD. \code{x} holds
the nodes in the coordinates of the SD's half-normal prior CDF (0 to 1).
\code{weights}, \code{density}, \code{cumulative}, and \code{log_normalizer} describe the
integrated SD distribution at those nodes. \code{nodes} holds the conditional
approximation at each node, with its \code{mode}, \code{covariance}, \code{hessian},
\code{log_density}, and optimizer \code{attempts}. The record also keeps
\code{prior_sd}, the grid \code{refinement} history, \code{converged}, \code{hyper_index}
(the position of the SD), \code{parameter_indices} (positions of node
parameters in the full layout), and the \code{draws}, \code{seed}, \code{cores},
\code{control}, and \code{coordinates} used. With one response grouping term
integrated inside an importance-corrected fit, the nodes exclude its
latent site effects. The log normalizer describes the numerical
integration. It is not a model-selection criterion.
\item \code{importance}: all retained production \code{candidates} (one row each) with
normalized \code{weights} and \code{log_weights} (log posterior minus log proposal
density), and the \code{resample_ids} into the candidates that built \code{draws}.
\code{history} has one row per production stage, with its diagnostics,
whether it \code{passed}, and whether it met the requested minimum
(\code{budget_met}). The record also keeps the \code{seed}, a text description of
the \code{proposal}, the \code{adapted_proposal} mixture (its \code{components} and
their \code{weights}, where each learned component counts in
\code{skipped_nodes} the nodes that kept their original geometry),
\code{pilot_candidates}, \code{proposal_indices}, the
\code{conditional_response} flag, \code{production_candidates} (including
discarded stages), \code{response_proposal} (fallback count and largest
remaining Newton step), and \code{proposal_diagnostics}, the original
marginal checks. \code{target_evaluation} counts every posterior density
evaluation (\code{evaluations}), invalid automatic-differentiation values
(\code{ad_invalid}), ordinary rechecks that recovered a finite or zero
density (\code{recovered_finite}, \code{recovered_zero}), and values left
\code{unresolved}, including pilots and discarded stages. Every candidate is
kept, and unresolved densities still fail the importance checks. With
\code{conditional_beta = TRUE} the record also holds \code{conditional_beta} and
\code{beta_proposal}, which gives the starting coefficients (\code{start}), the
number of \code{fallbacks} to the coefficients' normal prior, the largest
remaining Newton step (\code{max_step}), and the \code{latent_prior_fraction} of
the defensive component.
}
}

